# Entity-first file names, folders as units, modules on the gateway

## Pyramid Layer

- Layer: L0 convention.

## Use This When

- You are about to create a file or folder.
- A file becomes a folder of parts (see `file-size-tiers.mdc` for when).
- You add a module to the gateway, or import across two of its modules.

## Stop Here If

- The name reads `<name>.<role>.<ext>` and sits in the layer folder that owns it, and anything imported from a folder that has an `index.ts` comes through that index.

## Core rule

- File names are `<name>.<role>.<ext>`, the entity or component first: `profile.service.ts`, `profile.view.tsx`, `profile.shape.surql`.
- Fixed roles in a feature: `module`, `shape`, `commands`, `model.gen`, `service`, `machine`, `container`, `view`, `spec`, `view.spec`, `stories`, plus `entity`, `port`, `rule` (one domain rule function, `profile-name.rule.ts`) and `policy` (one application business function, `locale-change.policy.ts`).
- A component folder is kebab-case and has the component's name.
- Feature layout: `<feature>/<feature>.module.ts`, `domain/`, `application/`, `presentation/pages/<page>/`, `presentation/components/<name>/`, `presentation/i18n/`.
- A folder as a unit: when one file grows into several, they live in a folder named by what they share, without repeating that name inside (`region-sync/push.ts`, not `region-sync/region-push.ts`), with an `index.ts` that only re-exports the folder's public surface. A part keeps its role suffix when it has one (`members.view.tsx`, `invite.policy.ts`); a part that is an internal piece of one unit needs none (`push.ts`). Outside the folder, import the folder (`./region-sync`), never a file inside it. No folder for a single file.
- The gateway is built from modules the same way: one folder per mechanism of the platform under `apps/gateway/src/` (`auth/`, `sync/`, `tenants/`, `limits/`), each with an `index.ts` as the only entry point for the rest of the server, its internals private to it, and its own README (`package-readme.mdc`). A module grows `domain/` and `application/` folders once it holds domain rules, not before. The composition root stays the only place that assembles adapters.
- The index rule applies to folders that have an `index.ts`. Modules and folders that predate this rule have none and keep their flat layout: they are imported file by file, as today, until their next split gives them an index. A new module that needs something from such a module imports the file; it does not migrate the other module on the way.
- Tests sit next to the code they test.
